# Smart Restaurant API

A small REST API for managing a restaurant menu stored in PostgreSQL. It uses Node.js and Express.

## Requirements

- Node.js
- npm
- PostgreSQL

## Configure and run locally

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and set `DATABASE_URL` to your PostgreSQL connection string. For example:

```text
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:your_password@localhost:5432/food_ordering_db
PGSSL=false
PORT=3000
```

The database, user, and schema must already exist. On startup, the API checks its PostgreSQL connection but does not create or alter tables. It uses the supplied `menu_items` schema (`item_id`, `restaurant_id`, `category_id`, `base_price`, and `is_available`).

```bash
npm install
npm start
```

The server listens at `http://localhost:3000`. Visit `/` to confirm it is running.

## Menu endpoints

All menu routes are under `/api/menu`.

| Method | Path | Purpose | Success response |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `GET` | `/api/menu` | List, search, filter, and paginate items | `200 OK` |
| `GET` | `/api/menu/:id` | Get one item | `200 OK` |
| `POST` | `/api/menu` | Create an item | `201 Created` |
| `PUT` | `/api/menu/:id` | Replace an item's fields | `200 OK` |
| `PATCH` | `/api/menu/:id` | Update selected fields | `200 OK` |
| `DELETE` | `/api/menu/:id` | Mark an item unavailable | `204 No Content` |

### Menu item fields

Create and replace requests require these schema fields:

| Field | Type | Rules |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `restaurant_id` | integer | Positive restaurant ID |
| `category_id` | integer or `null` | Optional category belonging to that restaurant |
| `name` | string | Must not be empty or whitespace only |
| `base_price` | number | Must be nonnegative |
| `is_available` | boolean | Required |

`description` and `image_url` are optional and may be `null`.

Example create request:

```http
POST /api/menu
Content-Type: application/json
```

```json
{
  "restaurant_id": 1,
  "category_id": 2,
  "name": "BBQ Chicken Pizza",
  "description": "Smoky chicken and barbecue sauce",
  "base_price": 250,
  "image_url": null,
  "is_available": true
}
```

`PUT` replaces the item's fields, so send the required fields. `PATCH` accepts one or more schema fields and keeps the rest unchanged. For example:

```http
PATCH /api/menu/1
Content-Type: application/json
```

```json
{
  "base_price": 275
}
```

Names are trimmed before storage. Unknown fields are rejected. `DELETE` marks an item unavailable instead of removing a row, preserving menu history and references.

### List, filter, and search

`GET /api/menu` accepts these query parameters:

| Parameter | Behavior | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `restaurant_id` | Filter by restaurant | All restaurants |
| `category_id` | Filter by category ID | All categories |
| `search` | Case-insensitive substring match in item name or description | No search filter |
| `page` | Positive integer page number | `1` |
| `limit` | Positive integer page size | `10` |

Filters are applied before pagination. For example:

```http
GET /api/menu?restaurant_id=1&category_id=2&search=cheese&page=1&limit=5
```

The list response contains a `data` array and pagination metadata:

```json
{
  "data": [],
  "pagination": {
    "page": 1,
    "limit": 5,
    "total": 0,
    "totalPages": 0
  }
}
```

### Errors

- `400 Bad Request` — invalid item data, ID, or query parameter.
- `404 Not Found` — a valid ID does not match a menu item.
- `204 No Content` — item deleted successfully; response body is empty.

Error responses use this shape:

```json
{
  "message": "Menu item not found"
}
```

## Data storage

All menu reads and writes use PostgreSQL. Records persist across server restarts and are shared by API instances connected to the same database. The connection can also be configured with `PGHOST`, `PGPORT`, `PGDATABASE`, `PGUSER`, and `PGPASSWORD` instead of `DATABASE_URL`. Set `PGSSL=true` when your provider requires TLS.

The dependency list now includes `pg` (the PostgreSQL client) and `dotenv` (to load local `.env` settings). The previous project only depended on Express because it stored menu data in memory and had no database configuration.
